-- title:
--   Reduction into the second component equals Gauss reduction of σ̄ P
-- statement:
--   **Arithmetic data.** Fixed are a prime $p$, an integer $M \ge 5$ with $p \nmid M$ and $M \ne 0$, a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$. Further, $K$ is an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \operatorname{LaurentSeries} L$, and the hypothesis `hK` requires $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $\operatorname{LaurentSeries} L$ generated by the coefficientwise image under $\mathbb{Q} \to L$ of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Next, $A$ is a discrete valuation domain with $L$ as its fraction field, subject to `hAp` ($p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$) and `hζA` ($\zeta$ is in the image of $A \to L$), and $K$ is an $A$-algebra compatibly with $L$. Finally $j \in K$ is nonzero and, by `hj`, its underlying Laurent series is the coefficientwise image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) of the modular invariant.
--
--   The two-chart model [`ModularCurve.TwoChartModel A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L229) is the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135) (the elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$) and of `chartAlgInf` (the elements integral over $A[j^{-1}]$) along the common middle chart, with structure morphism [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, assumed proper; [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.ιFin A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L231) denotes the morphism from the finite chart into this model.
--
--   **The special fibre in characteristic $p$.** Here $k$ is an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and an $A$-algebra, and $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ are proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral. The data $i_1, i_2$ are morphisms over $k$ from $C_1$, $C_2$ to the base change `baseChange A (ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A ↥K j) k`, i.e. to the second projection of the pullback of `modelTo` along $\operatorname{Spec} k \to \operatorname{Spec} A$, and both are closed immersions. The hypothesis `hcover` requires every point of that pullback to lie in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` requires the fibre product of $i_1$ and $i_2$ to be reduced; `hn` and `hn0` provide a natural number $n > 0$ equal to the number of points of that fibre product.
--
--   **The generic fibre.** $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (taken as `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`) is an $A$-algebra and an $L$-algebra, compatibly. The datum $M_\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (the coefficientwise base change of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$): a proper smooth integral curve $M_\eta.C$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ together with an isomorphism $M_\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}$ onto its function field and a bijection $M_\eta.\mathrm{placeOfPoint}$ from closed points to places, whence the equivalence $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}$ between $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points (sections of $M_\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$) and places. The morphism $e_\eta$ from $M_\eta.C$ to the pullback of `modelTo` along $\operatorname{Spec} A \leftarrow \operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is an isomorphism and, by `heη`, followed by the second projection it is $M_\eta.\mathrm{toBase}$. The instance `Mη_chart_nonempty` asserts that the preimage under $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection of the open image of the finite chart is nonempty. The reading pin `hMηpin` requires, for every $a$ in the finite chart algebra, that the element of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` obtained from $a$ by transporting the global section along the chart, pulling it back along $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection, taking its germ at the generic point and applying $M_\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}^{-1}$, has as underlying Laurent series the coefficientwise image under $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the Laurent series of $a$. The equivariance hypothesis `hgal` requires that for every $g \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M_\eta.C$, if $x'$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by $x$, $e_\eta$ and the first projection, then $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x' = \mathrm{arithmeticGalois}(\mathrm{x1FunctionField}(Mp))(g) \cdot M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x$, the action being by the semilinear automorphism acting coefficientwise through $g$.
--
--   **The Igusa field and its two models.** The datum $w$ is a [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16): a weight-one modular form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ together with an integral power series realising its $q$-expansion whose image in $k$ is nonzero; [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35) is the field obtained from the $k$-function field of $X_1(M)$ by adjoining the inverse of that image. Two `CurveModel`s $\mathrm{Mdl}_1, \mathrm{Mdl}_2$ over $k$ of this field are given, with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ and $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ compatible with the structure morphisms (`he₁`, `he₂`), together with the nonemptiness instances `hne₁`, `hne₂` for the corresponding finite-chart preimages. The Gauss reading pin `hgauss₁` requires: for $a$ in the finite chart algebra and $x, y \in A[[q]]$ with $y$ having nonzero image in $k[[q]]$ and with $a \cdot y_L = x_L$ as Laurent series over $L$, the element of the Igusa field obtained by transporting $a$ along $e_1$ followed by $i_1$ and the first projection, then taking the germ at the generic point, reads as the quotient $\bar x / \bar y$ of Laurent series over $k$. The twisted pin `hgauss₂` is the same statement for $\mathrm{Mdl}_2$, $e_2$, $i_2$, with the defining relation imposed on $\sigma(a)$ instead of $a$: if $\sigma(a) \cdot y_L = x_L$, then the transported germ reads as $\bar x / \bar y$. Finally $\mathrm{frobIg}$ is an element of $\mathrm{SemilinearAut}\,k$ of the Igusa field (a pair of ring automorphisms compatible with $k \to$ Igusa field) whose action, by `hfrobIg`, raises every Laurent coefficient to the $p$-th power.
--
--   **The automorphism $\sigma$ and its avatar $\bar\sigma$.** $\sigma$ is an $L$-algebra automorphism of $K$ with: `hσj`, the Laurent series of $\sigma(j)$ is the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.qExpand ℚ p ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25), i.e. $j(q^p)$; `hσfin`, for $b \in K$ one has $b$ in the finite chart algebra (elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$) if and only if $\sigma b$ is; and `hσW`, for every valuation subring $W_0$ of $K$ which consists exactly of those $f$ admitting $x, y \in A[[q]]$ with nonzero residual $y$ and $f \cdot y_L = x_L$ — the Gauss valuation ring — the pullback of $W_0$ along $\sigma$ differs from $W_0$, and for every $P \in A[X]$ with nonzero reduction both $P(j)$ and $P(j)^{-1}$ lie in that pullback. The automorphism $\bar\sigma$ is a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` which, by `hσbar`, is compatible with $\sigma$: whenever $f$ has as Laurent series the coefficientwise image of that of $b \in K$, the Laurent series of $\bar\sigma f$ is the coefficientwise image of that of $\sigma b$.
--
--   **Reduction data.** $\mathrm{Pl}$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit in it (`hPl`), $\rho : A \to \mathrm{Pl}$ lifts $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρ`), $O$ is a subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $O \le \mathrm{Pl}$ (`hO`) and $\mathrm{Pl} \le O$ (`hOPl`), $\rho_O : A \to O$ again lifts $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρO`), and $\pi_k : \mathrm{Pl} \to k$ is a surjective (`hπk`) ring homomorphism with $A \to k$ equal to $\pi_k \circ \rho$ (`hAlgk`). Two maps $\mathrm{red}_1, \mathrm{red}_2$ from places of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to places of the Igusa field over $k$ are given, characterised by `hred₁` and `hred₂` respectively: for a place $P$, a morphism $\xi : \operatorname{Spec} O \to \mathrm{TwoChartModel}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho_O)$ and a $k$-point $c$ of $C_1$ (resp. $C_2$), if $\operatorname{Spec}(O \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by $\xi$ equals the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}^{-1}(P)$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection, and if $c$ followed by $i_1$ (resp. $i_2$) and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k \circ (O \hookrightarrow \mathrm{Pl}))$ followed by $\xi$, then $\mathrm{red}_1(P)$ (resp. $\mathrm{red}_2(P)$) is the place attached by $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}$ (resp. $\mathrm{Mdl}_2.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}$) to the $k$-point $c$ followed by $e_1^{-1}$ (resp. $e_2^{-1}$).
--
--   **Hecke hypotheses.** `hβdef` requires $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\mathbb{Q},p}(y)$ to lie in `x1x0FunctionFieldC ℚ (M*p) (M*p*p)` for every $y$ in the function field of $X_1(Mp)$; `hα` and `hβ` require the ring homomorphisms underlying `heckeAlphaOneBar` and `heckeBetaOneBar` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ to be integral; an instance provides principal divisors for the base-changed $X_1/X_0$ function field; and `hdeg` requires `finrankAlong` of `heckeBetaOneBar (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (M*p) p`, the dimension of the target over the source through that map, to equal $p$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For all places $P, P_1$ of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $P_1 = \mathrm{SemilinearAut.ofAlgAut}(\bar\sigma) \cdot P$, if there exist a morphism $\xi : \operatorname{Spec} O \to \mathrm{TwoChartModel}$ over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho_O)$ and a $k$-point $c$ of $C_2$ such that
--
--   (i) $\operatorname{Spec}(O \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ followed by $\xi$ equals $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}^{-1}(P)$ followed by $e_\eta$ and the first projection;
--
--   (ii) $c$ followed by $i_2$ and the first projection equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k \circ (O \hookrightarrow \mathrm{Pl}))$ followed by $\xi$;
--
--   (iii) for every point $t$ of the source of $c$, the image $c(t)$ does not lie in the range of the base map of the second projection of the fibre product of $i_1$ and $i_2$;
--
--   (iv) for every such $t$, the image of $t$ under the base map of $c$ followed by $i_2$ and the first projection lies in the range of the base map of [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.ιFin A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L231);
--
--   then $\mathrm{red}_2(P) = \mathrm{red}_1(P_1)$.
--
--   This is the transport step comparing the two reduction maps attached to the two components of the special fibre at $p$ of the modular curve of level $Mp$: a place of the generic fibre whose reduction lands on the second component, away from the intersection with the first component and inside the finite chart, has the same reduction there as the $\bar\sigma$-translate of that place has on the first component, where $\sigma$ is the automorphism sending $j(q)$ to $j(q^p)$. It is used in the subsequent derivation of the relation between the reduction maps, the Frobenius of the Igusa curve and the diamond automorphisms.
